Review this CUDA kernel used in a temperature simulation. It is correct for even block sizes but returns wrong sums for odd ones, and the profiler shows a large tail effect.

```cuda
__global__ void block_sum(const float * in, float * out, int n) {
    extern __shared__ float s[];
    int t = threadIdx.x;
    s[t] = in[blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + t];
    __syncthreads();
    for (int stride = blockDim.x / 2; stride > 0; stride >>= 1) {
        if (t < stride) s[t] += s[t + stride];
        __syncthreads();
    }
    if (t == 0) out[blockIdx.x] = s[0];
}

block_sum<<<grid, 256, 256 * sizeof(float)>>>(d_in, d_out, n);
```

The host code launches grid = (n + 255) / 256 without guarding the last block, and the call sites pass n values that are not multiples of 256. Explain the out-of-bounds read, the odd-size failure, and the tail effect, then rewrite the kernel with a bounds check and a warp-shuffle reduction. State the occupancy and bank-conflict implications of your version.

Explain this C++20 compile error and give the smallest correct fix.

```cpp
#include <concepts>
#include <vector>
#include <ranges>

template <std::integral T>
struct histogram {
    std::vector<T> bins;
    explicit histogram(std::size_t n) : bins(n, T{}) {}
    void add(T v) { ++bins[static_cast<std::size_t>(v)]; }
};

template <std::ranges::input_range R>
auto counts(R&& r, std::size_t n) {
    histogram h(n);
    for (auto&& v : r) h.add(v);
    return h;
}
```

gcc 14 reports "class template argument deduction failed" at the histogram declaration, and clang 18 additionally reports that the constraint on add is not satisfied when the range yields a reference. The intent is that counts works for any integral input range and for standard containers. Explain the deduction failure, the reference problem, and the fix, and note whether a deduction guide or an explicit template argument is the better design.
